Z||000001||Monkeyman||09-10-2000||10:45 PM||||I thought we were supposed to use one out of a FWD car, not RWD.  Or am I confused???||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||09-10-2000||10:59 PM||||The RWD 3.4 is an iron head engine, and the Fiero externals will fit with no major modifications. The main thing is the starter will need to be relocated to the other side of the block. I would "assume" that a FWD 3.4 would be an aluminum head engine and require use of the donor car's ECM and management harness to work. If someone could/would create an upper intake manifold for the AL head engines so a distributer would fit, that could be a blessing to us Fiero folks. I would love to have one of those roller cam, GEN 2 head 3.4's in my GT! Hmmm, maybe some creative TIG welding is in order. . . .||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||09-11-2000||01:15 AM||||As Cooter stated, the RWD 3.4 will accept all of the Fiero stuff.....intake, etc. with no major problems.  The only caveat is you need to redrill the starter holes on the opposite side of the block.  This is what Ed Parks, Bob Steiger and a host of other shops do.<P>The FWD 3.4 has aluminum heads and isn't just a bolt on thing....lots of rewiring, etc.  However.....if you planned on rebuilding the engine anyways the FWD would be preferable since the starter holes are already on the correct side.  You will need to use the Fieros heads and get flat top pistons (the aluminum head 3.4s have dished pistons) to make it all work.  <P>If you were rebuilding the motor then this wouldn't be a big deal, but if you wanted to buy a low mileage 3.4 to install then you'll need a RWD one and have the block drilled.    <P>Whew!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||09-11-2000||01:49 AM||||So what vehicles does the FWD 3.4 blobk come out of.  If I can get a decent block for cheap I wouldn't mind the rebuild, at least that way I can get the compression ration I want for a possible future forced induction installation.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||09-11-2000||12:41 PM||||Late model Grand Ams and mini-vans (Montana, etc.) have the 3.4 engine.  ||||reg||1
